PROPRIETARY SOFTWARE — NOT OPEN SOURCE

This repository and everything in it — source code, configuration, database
migrations, seed data, documentation and the published policy content — is
proprietary and confidential. It is made available to named recipients under a
separate written agreement and for no other purpose.

WHAT IS NOT GRANTED

No licence is granted to any person obtaining a copy of this software to use,
copy, modify, merge, publish, distribute, sublicense, create derivative works
of, or sell copies of it, in whole or in part, except as expressly permitted in
writing by the copyright holder. Absence of a technical restriction is not a
grant of permission.

This applies to every workspace in the monorepo. All twenty-eight manifests — the
root, the packages under packages/, the applications under apps/ and the build
gates under tools/ — carry "license": "UNLICENSED", npm's registered spelling for
proprietary software that grants no rights, and all twenty-eight also set
"private": true. One identifier, so a licence audit reading this workspace gets
one answer rather than a discrepancy to chase.

The npm form "SEE LICENSE IN LICENSE" is deliberately not used, including at the
root. It is resolved relative to the directory of the manifest that uses it, so
in packages/clock it would name packages/clock/LICENSE — a file that does not
exist and should not, since none of these packages is published to a registry,
packed, or distributed on its own. This file is the single place the terms are
written and maintained; "UNLICENSED" in a manifest says that no rights are
granted, and this document says on what terms a named recipient may nonetheless
hold the code.

WHAT THIS DOCUMENT IS NOT

This is the licence for the *source code*. It is not the agreement under which
anyone uses the hosted product — that is the terms of service, published at
/legal/terms and authored in packages/trust/src/content.ts. It is not a privacy
notice either; that is /legal/privacy. Where this document and a signed customer
agreement differ on the same point, the signed agreement governs the product and
this document continues to govern the code.

THIRD-PARTY COMPONENTS

Compass depends on open-source software, each component under its own licence.
Those licences are unaffected by this one and continue to govern their own code;
nothing here purports to relicense them. The dependency inventory is generated
as an SBOM — see docs/ and tools/quality-gates/tests/sbom-documentation.test.ts
for how it is produced and what it must contain.

